- As of today Summit Software holds $1.20M in cash and is burning ~$45K/month. At that burn level we cover roughly 26.7 months of runway on a cash-only basis. The company reported $4.0M revenue last quarter (≈$1.33M/month), so operating cash burn is a very small share (~3.4%) of recent revenue. If the company’s internal runway estimate of 30 months reflects additional expected inflows (AR collections, recurring revenue, or forecasted margin improvements), that implies an effective net outflow closer to $40K/month.

Key calculations and assumptions (use these verbatim if you want)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Reported burn rate: $45,000 / month → $540,000 / year
- Runway (cash-only, simple): 1,200,000 ÷ 45,000 = 26.67 months (≈26.7 months)
- Revenue last quarter: $4,000,000 → monthly average = 4,000,000 ÷ 3 = $1,333,333
- Burn as % of monthly revenue: 45,000 ÷ 1,333,333 = 3.375% (so cash burn is a low share of recent revenue)
- Reconciliation note: Your 30-month runway figure implies an effective net monthly outflow of 1,200,000 ÷ 30 = $40,000/month. If that’s the published runway, state the components that bring burn from $45K → $40K (e.g., expected collections, temporary cost recoveries, or one-off inflows).

Narrative on burn trend (suggested wording)
- “Our cash position is strong relative to current operating needs. At a $45K monthly cash burn, we have roughly 26.7 months of runway based purely on cash on hand. Importantly, last quarter revenue of $4.0M (≈$1.33M/month) dwarfs our cash outflows — cash burn represents just ~3.4% of recent monthly revenue — indicating that our operating model is generating substantial top-line cash inflows compared to discretionary cash usage. The finance team’s 30‑month runway projection incorporates expected working-capital collections and forecasting adjustments, which lowers the net monthly cash outflow to about $40K/month. We will continue to monitor collections and margin trends monthly and update the runway as actual cash flows crystallize.”

Implications / talking points for leadership
- Strength: Very long runway relative to burn gives flexibility to invest in growth or product initiatives without immediate financing pressure.
- Exposure: Runway assumes current burn and any stated forecasted inflows — changes in sales cadence, large one-time investments, or unexpected churn could shorten runway quickly, so maintain monthly monitoring.
- Leverage: Given high revenue relative to burn, focus on margin expansion and collection efficiency to convert revenue to cash faster.
- Messaging: For external stakeholders, emphasize the multi-year runway and healthy revenue trajectory; for internal stakeholders, stress disciplined spend and monthly reforecasting.

Sensitivity scenarios (quick numbers you can cite)
- Baseline: $45K/month burn → 1.2M / 45K = 26.7 months
- If burn increases to $75K/month → runway = 1.2M / 75K = 16 months
- If burn decreases to $30K/month → runway = 1.2M / 30K = 40 months
- If runway target is 30 months → implied net burn = 1.2M / 30 = $40K/month (use this to explain the 30-month figure)

Recommended next steps (operational & reporting)
- Reconcile the 30-month runway: explicitly document the items included (AR, forecasted revenue, expected cost reductions, cash commitments) so internal/external stakeholders understand assumptions.
- Monthly cash roll-forward: show opening cash, operating cash flow, financing, and closing cash each month; update runway each month.
- Prepare a 3-scenario forecast for the board: base, downside (e.g., 20% drop in quarterly revenue), upside (revenue growth / cost improvements).
- Convert revenue → cash KPI: track DSO / cash conversion to ensure revenue actually converts to cash; accelerate collections where possible.
- Hold discretionary spending and hiring until new forecasts are reviewed if you see early signs of revenue softness.

Suggested visuals for the slide deck
- Cash runway chart (months on x-axis): baseline and two sensitivity lines (burn +25%, -33%).
- Monthly cash flow waterfall for the last 6–12 months (opening cash → operating cash flow → investing/financing → closing cash).
- Burn vs revenue bar chart (quarterly revenue vs quarterly cash burn).
- Table: assumptions row (cash, burn, revenue, implied net burn, runway).

Optional: CFO talking points (short)
- “We have $1.2M cash and a conservative burn of $45K/month, which yields ~27 months runway on a cash-only basis. With $4M revenue last quarter, cash burn is only ~3.4% of revenue. Our 30‑month internal runway incorporates expected inflows and results in an implied net outflow of ~$40K/month. We will continue monthly reforecasting and scenario planning to ensure runway remains sufficient as we execute our growth plan.”

