Summit’s cash position is healthy relative to current operating needs. With $1.2M on the balance sheet and a modest net outflow of roughly $45K per month, we have nearly two-plus years of runway under current assumptions. Given last quarter’s revenue of $4.0M, the net cash burn represents a small fraction of our top line — indicating strong operating leverage or high margins in the recent period. This affords management flexibility to invest in priority initiatives (product, sales expansion, targeted hiring) without immediate fundraising pressure.

That said, runway is sensitive to changes in either revenue or operating spend. A sustained decline in sales or a step-up in investment hiring/one-time spends could materially shorten runway. We should therefore (a) re-confirm the runway calculation and any assumptions (deferred revenue draws, receivables timing, one-off liabilities), (b) stress-test 3 scenarios (base, downside, upside) over the next 12–24 months, and (c) track leading indicators (monthly recurring revenue, large deal pipeline conversion, DSO, and hiring/contractor commitments) to catch inflection points early.

5) Scenario math (illustrative — include in slide appendix)
