Executive summary (short, board-style)
Summit Software is holding $1.2M in cash. Based on our most recent runway calculation of 30 months, we are operating with an implied net cash burn of $40k/month. However, the operating burn we track in monthly reports is $45k/month, which would reduce runway to ~26.7 months. Revenue for the most recent quarter was $4.0M (≈$1.33M/month; annualized ≈$16M), but revenue recognition and cash collection timing mean strong top-line performance does not automatically translate to increased cash balances. We recommend a quick reconciliation of cash flow drivers (collections, capex, one-time uses) and a rolling 12‑month cash forecast under three scenarios to update runway and action items for preserving optionality.

Supporting calculations and metrics (for slides or appendix)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter average): 4.0M / 3 = $1,333,333 / month
- Annualized revenue (simple): 4.0M * 4 = $16.0M / year
- Implied burn from runway: 1.2M / 30 = $40k / month
- If burn = $45k / month → runway = 1.2M / 45k = 26.67 months
- Sensitivity (runway in months = cash / monthly burn):
  - Burn = $45k → runway ≈ 26.7 months
  - Burn = $40k → runway = 30.0 months
  - Burn = $36k (20% reduction) → runway ≈ 33.3 months
  - Burn = $49.5k (10% increase) → runway ≈ 24.2 months

Interpretation / narrative points to include
- High revenue vs. relatively low cash: The $4M quarterly revenue is strong, but cash is low relative to revenue because revenue recognition ≠ cash collections. Call out potential drivers: AR days / payment terms (large contracts invoiced but not yet collected), deferred revenue accounting, timing of milestone payments, customer concentration, or substantial outflows (one-time capex, M&A, tax, or contractor payouts).
- Burn definition: Be explicit whether “burn” is net cash outflow (cash from ops + investing + financing) or just operating cash burn (payroll, rent, opex). Use “net cash burn” in external/internal slides.
- Runway sensitivity: Present runway banding (best / base / downside) instead of a single static number. Show the impact of modest cost reductions or collection improvements on runway months.
- Trend: If burn has been stable at $45k, runway is ~26.7 months and still healthy. If runway of 30 months was communicated as a certainty, clarify that it likely used a different burn base (e.g., normalized or excluding one-time cash uses).

Suggested Board/Finance narrative (ready-to-use)
“Current cash on hand is $1.2M. Using our latest reported net cash burn, our runway is in the 27–30 month range depending on the burn metric applied. Our reported monthly burn on the P&L is $45k (which implies ~26.7 months of runway), while the 30-month runway figure implies an adjusted monthly burn of $40k. Revenue in Q[ ] was $4.0M (≈$1.33M/month; annualized ≈$16M), but collections and timing differences mean top-line strength has not fully converted into cash reserves. We will finalize an updated 12‑month cash flow forecast this week to reconcile AR, deferred revenue, and one-off outflows and will present three scenarios (base, downside, and upside) with recommended mitigations to extend runway and preserve strategic optionality.”

Recommended next steps (operational & financial)
1. Reconcile immediately (urgent, within 48–72 hours)
   - Confirm whether the 30-month runway used $40k/month or whether it omitted one-time outflows.
   - Produce a cash bridge for the past 6 months showing cash at beginning, cash from operations, investing, financing, and ending cash.
2. Cash flow forecast (deliverable within 5 business days)
   - Build a 12-month rolling cash forecast (monthly) with three scenarios: base (current), downside (-10–20% revenue / +10% cost), and upside (improved collections / 10% cost reduction).
   - Include AR aging and expected collection timings, scheduled capex, and any debt servicing.
3. Operational actions to extend runway (if desired)
   - Accelerate collections: tighten payment terms, offer discounts for early payment, prioritize large invoices.
   - Trim discretionary spend: hiring freeze, pause non-essential projects, renegotiate vendor terms.
   - Consider short-term financing or a bridge if a large growth opportunity requires capital.
4. Reporting & KPIs to include in internal materials
   - Cash balance, net cash burn (monthly), runway (months) with assumptions, revenue (quarter & month), AR days, deferred revenue, gross margin, and one-time cash items called out separately.

Optional sensitivity examples (to illustrate in a slide)
- If we reduce monthly burn by 10% (45k → 40.5k): runway extends to 1.2M / 40.5k ≈ 29.6 months.
- If we improve collections such that net cash inflow from operations increases by $50k/month, runway would expand rapidly (and could convert to positive net cash flow).

Closing / offer
