- Reconcile with reported runway: “Finance’s current runway figure of 30 months reflects [explain: e.g., expected collections of $X, one-time non-recurring expenses removed, or committed financing of $Y]. If we include those items, the effective monthly net burn used in that calculation is $40K/month.”
- Revenue context: “Revenue was $4.0M in the last quarter (≈ $1.33M/month). Important for the cash story: a large portion of this revenue may be invoiced and not yet collected or recognized in cash — we should disclose our AR days / cash collection lag to give the full picture.”
- Drivers and trend: “The low net burn relative to revenue indicates strong operating leverage. Key drivers affecting our burn trend are: payroll and hiring plan, variable sales and delivery costs tied to revenue, one-time investments (product or infrastructure), and timing of customer collections (DSO).”
- Risks & sensitivities: “Runway is highly sensitive to: (1) a sudden deterioration in cash collections (e.g., longer DSO), (2) planned hiring or product investment increases, and (3) large one-off expenditures. A 20% increase in net burn (to $54K/month) reduces runway to ~22 months; a 20% reduction (to $36K) extends runway to ~33 months.”
- Recommendation / next steps: “We should present a reconciled cash runway table in the deck: (a) headline runway based on current net cash burn, (b) reconciled runway that explains any adjustments (receivables, one-offs, committed financing), and (c) three scenarios (base, downside, upside) with clear assumptions on revenue collections and hiring. Also include KPIs: monthly net cash burn, gross burn, cash collections, DSO, and runway months.”

Short scenario examples (numbers)
- Base (use current net burn = $45K): runway = 1,200,000 / 45,000 = 26.7 months (~27 months).
- Cost reduction (20% lower burn → $36K): runway = 1,200,000 / 36,000 = 33.3 months (~33 months).
- Faster spend (50% higher burn → $67.5K): runway = 1,200,000 / 67,500 = 17.8 months (~18 months).
- Add financing (e.g., $2M raise): new cash = 3.2M → runway at $45K burn = 71.1 months (~71 months).

Suggested slide layout / table to include
- Row 1: Cash on hand
- Row 2: Net monthly cash burn (current)
- Row 3: Headline runway (cash / net burn)
- Row 4: Reconciliation items (expected cash in next 12 months, AR timing, one-offs)
- Row 5: Reconciled runway (after adjustments)
- Row 6: Scenario rows (Downside, Base, Upside) with assumptions and resulting runway
- KPIs column: DSO, Gross burn, Net burn, Monthly cash collections

