Thanks — I can help. First, a quick sanity check and then a set of polished narrative options you can drop into internal materials (exec summary, slide copy, and Q&A talking points). I’ll also flag assumptions you should confirm.

Quick numbers (transparent math)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Reported burn: $45,000 / month
- Runway (cash ÷ burn) = $1,200,000 ÷ $45,000 ≈ 26.7 months (≈ 27 months)
- Revenue last quarter: $4,000,000 → monthly revenue ≈ $4,000,000 ÷ 3 = $1,333,333 → annualized run-rate ≈ $16M

Note on the 30-month runway you quoted
- The simple math above gives ~27 months. To reach 30 months with $1.2M cash implies an effective net burn of $1,200,000 ÷ 30 = $40,000/month. That means one of:
  - The reported $45k burn is “gross” (expenses) and you’re expecting ~$5k/month of net positive cash from operations (i.e., revenue minus operating cash costs) that reduces net burn to $40k; OR
  - The 30 months is an estimate that rounds up or includes committed future revenue/capital or one-time adjustments.
- Please confirm whether $45k is net burn (after revenue) or gross spend before revenue — I can reconcile the exact narrative once you confirm.

Suggested finance narrative (ready-to-use)

1) Executive summary (2–3 lines)
Summit Software enters the period with a strong liquidity position: $1.2M of cash and a low monthly net burn of $45k. This implies roughly 27 months of runway on a cash-only basis, and a much longer effective horizon when we factor in our current revenue run-rate (Qtr revenue $4.0M, annualized ~$16M). We are cash-healthy and well-positioned to fund near-term product investments while evaluating targeted growth spending.

2) Slide copy — “Cash & Runway”
- Cash balance: $1.2M
- Monthly net burn: $45k
- Runway (cash ÷ burn): ≈ 27 months (1.2M / 45k)
- Revenue context: Last quarter revenue: $4.0M (monthly ≈ $1.33M; annualized ≈ $16M)
- Takeaway: At current burn rates and revenue trajectory, liquidity is sufficient to support operations and measured investments; maintain discipline while pursuing prioritized growth initiatives.

3) Slide copy — “Burn trend & drivers” (use alongside a 6–12 month trend chart)
- Current net burn is small relative to revenue — primarily reflects deliberate cost control and efficiency in ops.
- Primary drivers of burn:
  - Fixed operational overhead (hosting, contracts): [insert % or $ if available]
  - People costs (product & support): [insert]
  - Discretionary growth spend (sales/marketing, R&D pilot projects): [insert]
- Recent trend: [placeholder — e.g., “Burn down 10% vs. prior quarter due to reduced contractor spend” or “Stable over last 6 months”]. (I can fill this if you supply monthly burn history.)
- Action items: Continue strict prioritization of new hires and marketing spend; re-evaluate discretionary projects quarterly.

4) Risks & sensitivity (brief)
- Downside: A sustained increase in monthly burn (e.g., hiring spree or one-off legal/contract costs) would shorten runway proportionally. Example: increasing burn to $90k/month halves runway to ~13 months.
- Revenue shock: Given monthly revenue (~$1.33M), material revenue declines would be required before liquidity becomes an immediate concern — still, monitor customer churn and large client payment timing.
- Recommendation: Maintain 3–6 month contingency plan, and run scenario analyses for 10%, 25%, and 50% expense increases and for revenue shocks.

5) Recommended asks / follow-ups for leadership
- Confirm whether the $45k figure is net burn (after revenue) or gross spend.
- Provide monthly burn history for the last 6–12 months so we can show trend and explain drivers.
- Approve a short scenario table to include in the deck (Base / Conservative / Aggressive) showing runway under different assumptions.

Short talking points for Q&A
- “Our cash position ($1.2M) and low net burn (~$45k/month) give us ~27 months of runway on a cash-only basis; factoring in current revenue run-rate further improves our liquidity profile.”
- “Burn is intentionally low relative to revenue as we prioritize margin expansion; any planned increases in growth spend will be staged and reviewed.”
- “We recommend running sensitivity scenarios (e.g., +25% burn, -20% revenue) and a monthly burn trend chart to illustrate momentum and key cost drivers.”

If you want, I can:
- Draft the exact slide text and speaker notes for one or two slides.
- Produce a 3-scenario runway table (Base / Downside / Upside) with numbers.
- Reconcile the 30-month figure once you confirm whether $45k is net burn and whether the 30 months includes expected net operating cash inflows.

Which would you like me to do next? If you want the slides/text, tell me whether to (a) use 27 months (cash-only) or (b) use 30 months and include an explanatory footnote.

