Suggested finance narrative (ready to paste into presentations or reports)
Executive summary
- As of [report date] Summit Software holds $1.2M in cash. At our reported net cash burn of $45K/month, our on-hand cash supports approximately 26.7 months of runway. This gives the company more than two years of runway at current operating cadence, providing room to execute our growth and product milestones while we scale revenue.
Cash position and runway
- Cash on hand: $1.2M.
- Reported net cash burn: $45K/month.
- Simple runway calculation: 1,200,000 / 45,000 = 26.7 months. Note: this assumes burn remains steady and excludes any episodic one‑time items, planned investments, or new financing.
Revenue context and implications
- Revenue last quarter was $4.0M (annualized ≈ $16M). The magnitude of revenue relative to our current net burn indicates a strong top-line contribution; however, revenue recognition does not necessarily equal cash collected (monitor AR and deferred revenue). If operating cash conversion from revenue remains strong, net burn could decline materially and extend runway further.
Burn trend and risk factors
- If burn remains stable at $45K/month, cash declines linearly and reaches zero in ~26.7 months. Example checkpoints:
  - After 12 months: 1.2M − (12 × 45K) = $660K remaining
  - After 24 months: $120K remaining
  - Runout ≈ month 26–27
- Material risks to this runway include: temporary revenue shortfalls or collections delays (higher DSO), one-off capital expenditures, increased hiring or marketing spend, and underestimated operating expenses. Conversely, sustained revenue growth or improved collections will materially extend runway.
Scenario sensitivity (illustrative)
- Base (current): burn = 45K → runway = 26.7 months.
- Worse case (20% higher burn → 54K): runway = 1.2M / 54K = 22.2 months.
- Better case (20% lower burn → 36K): runway = 1.2M / 36K = 33.3 months.
- To hit exactly 30 months with current burn, cash required = 30 × 45K = $1.35M.
Action items & recommendations
1. Reconcile runway inputs: confirm whether the stated 30 months includes expected revenue or financing; confirm that $45K is net cash burn. Fix the official numbers before external/internal distribution.
2. Produce a monthly 12–24 month cash-flow forecast with scenarios (base, downside, upside). Include: opening cash, monthly cash receipts (sales collections), payroll & benefits, COGS, operating expenses, capex, taxes, and financing activity.
3. Stress-test collections: show the impact of longer AR days (DSO) on cash and runway.
4. Prepare a one-page slide showing: current cash, calculated runway (with formula), revenue last quarter, and 3 scenario lines (base, +20% burn, −20% burn) of cash balance by month.
5. Maintain a 3–6 month liquidity buffer and identify quick levers to preserve runway (temporary hiring pause, discretionary spend cuts, push-outs of noncritical capex).
Suggested slide copy / talking points (concise)
- Slide headline: “Cash & Runway — Current Position”
- Bullets:
  - Cash on hand: $1.2M
  - Net cash burn: $45K / month
  - Calculated runway: 26.7 months (1.2M ÷ 45K)
  - Revenue (last quarter): $4.0M — strong topline; monitor AR conversion to cash
  - Key ask: confirm whether cash/runway should incorporate expected receipts or financing; recommend approval to build monthly forecast and scenario analysis
Sample one-paragraph narrative for internal deck
- “As of [date], Summit Software has $1.2M of unrestricted cash. At our current net cash burn of approximately $45K per month, this equates to roughly 26.7 months of runway (1.2M ÷ 45K). Revenue last quarter was $4.0M; if cash collections and margins remain consistent, we expect net burn to decline and runway to extend. We recommend finalizing our monthly cash forecast and running downside scenarios (e.g., slower collections, hiring ramp) before committing to hires or major discretionary investments.”
