- Key takeaway: On a cash basis, Summit Software is well capitalized relative to current net burn (cash runway measured in multiple years). With quarterly revenue substantially larger than monthly cash burn, the immediate cash risk is low — but we should highlight working capital, seasonality, and concentration risks.

Cash position and short-term outlook
- Cash balance of $1.2M gives >2 years of runway even under conservative assumptions. Using the stated burn of $45K/month, runway = ~26.7 months. If management wants to present a rounded 30-month runway because near-term collections or timing items reduce net cash outflow, note that explicitly in the materials so the audience understands the basis.
- Collections and working capital are the primary operational cash drivers to monitor: with high quarterly revenue, delays in collections or large refunds could materially affect cash despite low monthly burn.
- Immediate action: maintain a rolling 12-month cash forecast updated weekly with actual receipts and vendor payment timing.

Burn trend and drivers (talking points)
- Historical: “Net cash burn has averaged $45K/month over the last [X] months, driven primarily by ongoing operating expenses (S&M, R&D, G&A).”
- Comparison to revenue: “Net burn is a small fraction of monthly revenue (≈3–4% if quarterly revenue is $4M), reflecting strong revenue generation relative to operating spend.”
- Drivers to call out:
  - Payroll and hiring cadence (largest driver of month-over-month changes)
  - One-time items (legal, restructuring, M&A-related fees)
  - Capex or product spend (timing can create temporary cash dips)
  - Changes in customer payment terms or refunds
