Thanks — I can help. A few important housekeeping points first, because your three inputs (cash, runway, burn) don’t align exactly and that affects the narrative.

Quick math check
- Reported cash: $1,200,000
- Reported burn: $45,000 / month
- 1,200,000 ÷ 45,000 = 26.7 months of runway (≈26 months 20 days), not 30 months.
- To have 30 months of runway at a $45K burn you would need 30 × 45,000 = $1,350,000 cash.
- To have 30 months from $1.2M cash the implied burn would be 1,200,000 ÷ 30 = $40,000 / month.

Please confirm which of these is correct: (A) burn = $45K/month and runway ≈ 26.7 months, (B) runway truly 30 months and burn ≈ $40K/month, or (C) burn is net of some future revenue collections (so runway calculation includes forecasted inflows). Also confirm what “revenue last quarter $4.0M” refers to (recognized revenue, cash collected, one-time vs recurring), and whether the stated burn is net burn (cash out - cash in) or gross burn (cash out only).

Suggested finance narrative (you can adapt depending on your clarification). I’ll give a short executive summary and a longer version for an internal slide or memo.

Executive summary (2–3 sentences)
Summit Software enters the period with $1.2M in cash and an effective monthly net burn of $45K, which provides roughly 27 months of runway at the current pace of spend. Last quarter revenue was $4.0M, which demonstrates strong top-line traction; the primary financial focus is converting revenue into cash collections and maintaining disciplined cost control to preserve runway while supporting growth initiatives.

Expanded narrative (3–4 paragraphs)
1) Current position and runway
As of today Summit holds $1.2M in cash. At our reported net burn of $45K per month (net cash outflow after operating receipts), this equates to approximately 26.7 months of runway. This runway calculation assumes no material changes to revenue collection timing, operating expenses, hiring plans, or one-time cash items. If the company’s runway is being presented as 30 months, that implies a lower monthly net burn (~$40K) or an expectation of near-term cash inflows not yet received — we should state that assumption explicitly.

2) Revenue context
Revenue of $4.0M last quarter (≈$1.33M/month) is a strong signal of demand, but revenue recognition does not automatically equate to cash in the bank. The key follow-up items to explain are (a) cash collections / DSO (days sales outstanding), (b) gross margin and contribution toward covering fixed costs, and (c) any large deferred revenues or refunds. If collections are timely, our strong revenue should reduce net burn; if collections are delayed or tied into long payment terms, our cash runway will be more dependent on the current cash balance and cost control.

3) Burn trend and drivers
Our reported net burn of $45K/month indicates we are operating close to cash neutrality relative to revenue — that’s an attractive profile. The burn trend over the past 6–12 months should show whether burn is increasing (ramping hires, higher opex) or decreasing (efficiency, margin improvements). Call out one-off items (e.g., product investments, marketing bursts, M&A costs) that have temporarily changed the burn rate and whether these are recurring.

4) Risks and mitigations
Primary risks: slower-than-expected cash collections, unplanned hiring or headcount rollback, major customer churn, or one-time capital needs. Mitigations: accelerate collections (incentives, stricter terms), tighten discretionary spend, pause non-critical hires, negotiate vendor payment terms, and plan a financing buffer (convertible or bridge) if fundraising is planned. If runway calculations incorporate projected revenue, make the assumptions explicit (timing, collection %, margin).

Concrete numbers and a simple projection you can include
- Constant-burn projection (no change to revenue collections; burn = $45K/month):
  - Cash today: $1,200,000
  - After 12 months: 1,200,000 – (45,000 × 12) = $660,000
  - After 24 months: 1,200,000 – (45,000 × 24) = $120,000
  - Breakeven cash: ≈26.7 months
  - After 30 months: deficit ≈ $150,000 (meaning runway exhausted ~3 months earlier than 30)
- If runway must be stated as 30 months, state the assumption explicitly (e.g., “Assumes burn reduces to $40K/month or $150K of expected collection within 30 days”).

Recommended slides/figures to include
- One-line cash runway with the math and assumptions (show both net burn and implied runway)
- Monthly cash balance projection (12–36 months) under base, upside, and downside scenarios
- Burn trend chart (gross and net burn over last 6–12 months)
- Revenue vs cash collected (monthly) to show conversion
- Key assumptions and sensitivities (e.g., 10% slower collections, 20% cost cut)

Suggested talking points for leadership
- “We have ~27 months of runway at current net burn; this gives us ample time to scale or optimize before raising.”
- “Revenue of $4M last quarter is a core strength; priority one is accelerating cash conversion to further expand runway.”
- “We should present 2–3 scenarios (base, conservative, aggressive) so stakeholders see how hiring, collections, or a fundraising event change runway.”

Immediate next steps I can do for you
- Draft a one-page finance memo summarizing the position with the corrected runway math and clear assumptions.
- Prepare a three-scenario cash projection (base / downside / upside) for a slide.
- Create the exact short slide text + speaker notes for the CFO.

Tell me which you want and confirm the ambiguity: is burn = net cash burn of $45K/month (and therefore runway ≈26.7 months), or should I treat runway = 30 months as the ground truth (and adjust the implied burn)? Also confirm if the $4.0M revenue was cash collected last quarter or revenue recognized (and any other upcoming one-time cash items).

