- Current monthly net cash burn: $45K.
- Runway (cash / burn): ~26.7 months at current burn.
- Revenue last quarter: $4.0M (note: revenue recognition and cash collection timing can materially affect cash runway).

Suggested text:
"At quarter end we hold $1.2M in cash with a current net cash burn of approximately $45K/month. On a cash-only basis, this implies roughly 26.7 months of runway at current spending levels. Revenue in the last quarter was $4.0M; however, timing of collections, margins, and working capital dynamics determine how much of that revenue converts to cash. We are continuing to monitor cash conversion and receivables closely, since those factors — not headline revenue alone — determine our near-term liquidity."

2) Adjusted narrative (if runway figure of 30 months already reflects expected revenue collections or other offsets)
Executive summary
- Cash on hand: $1.2M.
- Stated runway: 30 months (this implies an adjusted net burn of ~$40K/month).
- Revenue last quarter: $4.0M — if a portion of that revenue is expected to drive positive cash inflows over the forecast period, it reduces net burn and extends runway accordingly.

Suggested text:
"At quarter end we hold $1.2M in cash. With our planned operating profile and expected cash collections from recent revenue, our internal forecast shows approximately 30 months of runway (implying an adjusted net cash burn near $40K/month). Last quarter revenue was $4.0M; collection timing and margin assumptions are included in our forecast and materially improve runway versus a cash-only calculation. We continue to stress-test this assumption for collection risk and margin volatility."

3) Simple scenario analysis (for a one-slide summary)
- Base (use your stated burn of $45K/mo): runway = 1,200,000 / 45,000 = 26.7 months
- If burn reduced 10% (to $40.5K/mo): runway = 1,200,000 / 40,500 ≈ 29.6 months
- If burn increases 20% (to $54K/mo): runway = 1,200,000 / 54,000 ≈ 22.2 months
- Implied burn if runway = 30 months: burn = 1,200,000 / 30 = $40K/mo

Include these bullets in the slide so leadership sees sensitivity to small changes in monthly burn.

4) How to present burn trend (text + recommended visual)
- Visual: line chart — x-axis months (historic 12–18 months), y-axis monthly net cash burn; overlay 3-month moving average and a trendline. Add a second axis or small bar chart showing quarterly revenue for context.
- Narrative bullets:
  - "Burn has averaged $X/mo over the last 12 months, with [increase/decrease] driven by [headcount, product investment, ONE-OFF items]."
  - "Excluding one-time items, normalized cash burn is ~$YYK/month."
  - "Revenue growth last quarter was $4.0M; however, cash conversion (receivables days / refunds / deferred revenue) is the key driver of cash flow."

5) Key drivers & talking points to justify the numbers
- Drivers that reduce cash: payroll, contractor spend, R&D / product capex, marketing campaigns, rent and G&A, one-time restructuring or M&A costs.
- Drivers that improve cash: collections acceleration, increased gross margin, deferred vendor payments, seasonal revenue, one-off customer prepayments.
- If revenue is high but cash low, explain working capital: "Large revenue this quarter is largely subscription/recurring but with 60–90 day collections / significant refunds/deferred revenue, which postpones cash realization."

