Current position & interpretation
- Revenue vs. burn: On a straight comparison, last quarter’s monthly revenue of ~$1.33M far exceeds an operating net cash burn of $40–45K/month. That implies we are generating positive operating cash inflows after operating expenses (unless the revenue number is recognized revenue not yet collected, or there are significant non-cash items, deferred revenue movements, or large financing/one‑time cash outflows impacting position).
- Cash runway: If we treat burn as net monthly cash outflow (i.e., cash outflows minus cash inflows), then:
  - Burn = $45K → runway = 1.2M / 45K = 26.7 months.
  - Burn = $40K → runway = 1.2M / 40K = 30 months.
- Annualization: Last quarter revenue annualizes to ~ $16.0M. This scale versus $45K/month burn underscores that ongoing operations appear cash positive, but cash visibility hinges on collections, capex, and any irregular payments.

Drivers and trend analysis to explain to stakeholders
- Collections: If revenue is high but collections lag (high AR days), cash can still be tight. Reconcile DSO and collections timing.
- One-offs / seasonality: Identify any one-time cash items (taxes, bonuses, vendor prepayment, M&A / legal costs) that explain near-term variations.
- Deferred revenue and churn: If customers pay in advance, cash may look strong but revenue recognition will be spread; conversely, revenue recognized but not yet billed/collected can create mismatches.
- Capital needs: Any planned CapEx, product investments, or hiring plans that will materially change monthly cash burn should be called out.
- Non-cash items: Depreciation, stock comp, and accruals can make GAAP profit diverge from cash burn.

Scenario sensitivity (base / upside / downside) — assume current cash = $1.2M
- Base case (use stated burn $45K/mo): runway ≈ 26.7 months.
- Upside (reduce burn by 20% → $36K/mo): runway ≈ 33.3 months.
- Downside (increase burn by 25% → $56.25K/mo): runway ≈ 21.3 months.
- Revenue shock scenario (30% revenue drop, if revenue collections drop in cash): If collections fall and net burn flips to +$300K/month, runway would be 1.2M / 300K = 4 months — illustrate this to show sensitivity to revenue collections.

