"Summit enters the period with $1.2M in cash and a low net cash burn of $45K per month, which gives us approximately 26.7 months of runway at the current spend profile. Last quarter revenue was $4.0M, reflecting strong top-line performance and favorable operating leverage. With revenue at this run-rate and disciplined expense management, we have flexibility to selectively invest in product and go-to-market initiatives while maintaining multi-quarter liquidity. We will continue scenario planning to monitor runway sensitivity to hiring, marketing spend, and potential seasonality or customer concentration risks."

2) Detailed (3–4 paragraphs — for finance pack)
"Cash and runway: As of today we hold $1.2M in cash. At a net cash burn of $45K/month (our stated burn), the cash-only runway is ~26.7 months. If the 30-month runway number you provided is to be used, that implies a lower effective monthly burn (~$40K) or that the runway calculation already factors in projected inflows — please confirm.

Revenue context: Revenue last quarter was $4.0M (roughly $1.33M/month). If that sales run-rate continues, our revenue significantly exceeds current monthly burn, implying positive operating cash flow; therefore, it’s important we confirm whether the $45K burn figure is pre- or post-revenue. If $45K is the net cash outflow after revenue and collections, that already includes the benefit of revenue and is a conservative sign of strong cash conversion.

Trend and sensitivity: With a constant net burn of $45K/month, Summit spends $540K/year and would deplete $1.08M in two years. If burn increases by 25% (to ~$56K/mo) runway falls to ~21.3 months; if burn falls by 50% (to ~$22.5K) runway extends to ~53.3 months. We should model scenarios that include revenue variance (±20%), hiring plans, and one-off investments to show the full range of outcomes to the Board.

Risks & actions: Reconcile burn definition (gross vs net), confirm cash classification (restricted, deposits, etc.), surface any large receivables or deferred revenue that could affect cash timing, and prepare a 12–24 month cash forecast under three scenarios (base, conservative, growth). Recommended mitigations include phasing large hires, covenant monitoring, and a trigger-based plan for additional fundraising if runway drops below a predefined threshold (e.g., 12 months)."

